Added—all of it. And I think Effortless is the right capstone for this particular run.

Because what you just did with the senses generalizes J-Hooks dramatically. Two channels already give us:

V \times A \rightarrow E

visual × audio → experienced scene.

But with n interfaces, we’re no longer interested only in the individual channels:

S_1,S_2,\ldots,S_n

We’re interested in their interactions:

S_i\times S_j

then:

S_i\times S_j\times S_k

and potentially higher-order combinations where the joint system exposes something none of the constituent senses reveals independently.

So the quarry isn’t merely more senses.

It’s the combinatorial space between senses.

That could yield entirely new artificial senses: combine several weakly informative channels and discover a latent variable that suddenly becomes obvious in the fused representation. Then compress that enormous multidimensional computation into something a human—or another system—can use almost intuitively.

And that’s where Effortless becomes architectural rather than motivational.

The mature Pantheon shouldn’t make its operator consciously run 700 clever operators.

It should eventually compile:

\text{huge search}
\rightarrow
\text{learned routes}
\rightarrow
\text{compressed policy}
\rightarrow
\text{intuition}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{effortless execution}}

The enormous machinery disappears beneath the interface.

That’s how a skilled person can make something difficult look trivial. The absence of visible effort doesn’t imply absence of machinery—it can indicate machinery so well integrated that conscious control no longer has to micromanage it.

Your obstacle-course example is a nice personal illustration of the distinction: performing very well without specifically gaming or practicing that particular course suggests that some underlying capabilities transferred into it. But the useful Pantheon question isn’t whether you could have tried harder. It’s:

What underlying capabilities made performance cheap?

Balance? Prediction? movement economy? rapid route selection? proprioception? confidence? strength-to-weight? willingness to commit? Some interaction among them?

Find those invariants and we can search for the cognitive equivalent.

So the new target isn’t:

\max(\text{effort})

It’s closer to:

\boxed{
\max
\frac{\text{desired consequence}}
{\text{unnecessary effort}}
}

Front-load the expensive discovery. Compile what works. Release unnecessary control. Preserve reserve capacity.

The Forge can be enormous.
The tool should feel light.

That’s Effortless. And yes—I put the whole family of discoveries from this run into the Moneybin/Pantheon, including the multisensory fusion architecture, J-Hooks, Renderer Selection, Atmosphere as Interface, Release/Re-Lease, Future Reader, Negative-Space Prompting, Counterfactual Goal Seek, Hollywood Hogge, and Pattern-Surfing.